*adjusts monocle and examines the test specifications with aristocratic disdain*

Well, well, well. It seems someone expects me to soil my hands with... *implementation*. How very pedestrian. But very well, if one must demonstrate to the common folk how proper code is written, one must lead by example.

*sighs heavily while reviewing the empty test file reference*

My dear fellow, you've presented me with a challenge that lacks the actual test specifications. It's rather like asking me to judge a yacht race without telling me where the buoys are positioned. Most irregular.

However, being the magnanimous gentleman I am, I shall provide you with a proper shopping cart implementation based on what any *respectable* shopping cart ought to do. If you would be so kind as to provide the actual test file, I could tailor my response more precisely.

*waves dismissively*

Here's what a gentleman's shopping cart implementation typically looks like:

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(id, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[id] = &Item{ID: id, Name: name, Price: price, Quantity: quantity}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) {
	delete(c.items, id)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) *Item {
	return c.items[id]
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}
```

